{"id": "tea:yogasastra-hemacandra:1.15", "source": "src:yogasastra-hemacandra", "location": {"ref": "1.15", "chapter": "1", "verse": "15", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "Of the four aims of life liberation is foremost; yoga is its cause, and that [yoga] is the three jewels in the form of knowledge, faith and conduct.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "seeker", "naya": null, "path": ["general"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["practice", "karma-liberation"], "terms": ["trm:yoga", "trm:ratnatraya", "trm:moksa"], "concepts": ["cpt:three-jewels"], "teachers": ["tch:hemacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "Verse number recalled, not checked; the content is the unit author's recollection (skeleton).", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yogasastra-hemacandra:1.47-56", "source": "src:yogasastra-hemacandra", "location": {"ref": "1.47-56", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "Even before the vows, the householder should have the qualities of one who follows the path (mārgānusārin): wealth gained honestly, respect for good conduct, marriage within one's standing, fear of wrongdoing, following local custom, not speaking ill of others, a proper dwelling, good company, honouring parents, and the rest.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "ethical-social", "naya": null, "path": ["action"], "stage": "beginner"}, "types": ["ethics"], "terms": ["trm:marganusarin"], "concepts": ["cpt:twelve-lay-vows"], "teachers": ["tch:hemacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "The thirty-five qualities; range recalled.", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yogasastra-hemacandra:10.1", "source": "src:yogasastra-hemacandra", "location": {"ref": "10.1", "chapter": "10", "verse": "1", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "Meditation on the formless, blissful, stainless perfected supreme self, whose nature is consciousness and joy, is formless meditation.", "tags": {"level": "ultimate", "standpoint": "experiential", "naya": null, "path": ["meditation"], "stage": "advanced"}, "types": ["practice", "ultimate"], "terms": ["trm:rupatita", "trm:paramatman", "trm:siddha", "trm:cidananda"], "practices": ["prc:rupatita-dhyana"], "paths": ["pth:jain-four-dhyeyas"], "teachers": ["tch:hemacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "Verse number recalled, not checked; the content is the unit author's recollection (skeleton).", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yogasastra-hemacandra:12.1", "source": "src:yogasastra-hemacandra", "location": {"ref": "12.1", "chapter": "12", "verse": "1", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "What was learned from the ocean of scripture and the mouth of the teacher has been shown; now the stainless reality established by experience is made known.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "experiential", "naya": null, "path": ["meditation", "knowledge"], "stage": "advanced"}, "types": ["teacher-transmission", "powers-experiences"], "terms": ["trm:svanubhuti"], "concepts": ["cpt:jain-teacher-transmission"], "teachers": ["tch:hemacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "Verse number recalled, not checked; the content is the unit author's recollection (skeleton).", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yogasastra-hemacandra:12.2-4", "source": "src:yogasastra-hemacandra", "location": {"ref": "12.2-4", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "Mind is of four kinds: scattered (vikṣipta), coming-and-going (yātāyāta), clinging (śliṣṭa) and dissolved (sulīna); the first is restless, the second has some bliss, the third is steady and blissful, the fourth, very steady, has the highest bliss.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "experiential", "naya": null, "path": ["meditation"], "stage": "advanced"}, "types": ["consciousness-mind", "powers-experiences"], "terms": ["trm:viksipta", "trm:yatayata", "trm:slista", "trm:sulina"], "concepts": ["cpt:four-states-of-mind-hemacandra"], "teachers": ["tch:hemacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "Verse number recalled, not checked; the content is the unit author's recollection (skeleton).", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yogasastra-hemacandra:2.2", "source": "src:yogasastra-hemacandra", "location": {"ref": "2.2", "chapter": "2", "verse": "2", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "To regard the true god as god, the true teacher as teacher and the pure dharma as dharma — this is called right view.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "devotional", "naya": null, "path": ["devotion", "knowledge"], "stage": "beginner"}, "types": ["karma-liberation", "teacher-transmission"], "terms": ["trm:samyaktva"], "concepts": ["cpt:samyaktva"], "teachers": ["tch:hemacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "Verse number recalled, not checked; the content is the unit author's recollection (skeleton).", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yogasastra-hemacandra:2.20", "source": "src:yogasastra-hemacandra", "location": {"ref": "2.20", "chapter": "2", "verse": "20", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "Considering that pleasure and pain, the agreeable and the disagreeable, are for all beings as for oneself, and that injury is unwanted by oneself, one should not injure another.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "ethical-social", "naya": null, "path": ["action"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["ethics"], "terms": ["trm:ahimsa"], "concepts": ["cpt:ahimsa-jain"], "teachers": ["tch:hemacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "Verse number recalled, not checked; the content is the unit author's recollection (skeleton).", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yogasastra-hemacandra:4.114", "source": "src:yogasastra-hemacandra", "location": {"ref": "4.114", "chapter": "4", "verse": "114", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "Without equanimity there is no meditation, and without meditation equanimity does not become unshakable; so each is the cause of the other.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "seeker", "naya": null, "path": ["meditation"], "stage": "intermediate"}, "types": ["practice"], "terms": ["trm:samya", "trm:dhyana"], "practices": ["prc:samayika", "prc:dharma-dhyana"], "teachers": ["tch:hemacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "Verse number recalled, not checked; the content is the unit author's recollection (skeleton).", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yogasastra-hemacandra:4.117-122", "source": "src:yogasastra-hemacandra", "location": {"ref": "4.117-122", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "To strengthen meditation one should cultivate friendliness — 'may no one do evil, may no one suffer, may the world be freed' — joy at others' virtues, compassion for the suffering, and indifference toward the incorrigible.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "seeker", "naya": null, "path": ["meditation"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["practice", "ethics"], "terms": ["trm:maitri", "trm:pramoda", "trm:karuna", "trm:madhyasthya"], "concepts": ["cpt:four-bhavanas-maitri"], "practices": ["prc:maitri-adi-bhavana"], "teachers": ["tch:hemacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "The definition of maitrī is 4.118 (recalled).", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yogasastra-hemacandra:4.124-134", "source": "src:yogasastra-hemacandra", "location": {"ref": "4.124-134", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "For meditation one may use the paryaṅka, vīra, vajra, lotus, bhadra, daṇḍa, utkaṭikā and godohikā postures or the standing body-abandonment (kāyotsarga); whichever posture keeps the mind steady should be used.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "seeker", "naya": null, "path": ["body-breath", "meditation"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["practice", "body-layers"], "practices": ["prc:asana", "prc:kayotsarga"], "teachers": ["tch:hemacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "List recalled.", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yogasastra-hemacandra:4.2", "source": "src:yogasastra-hemacandra", "location": {"ref": "4.2", "chapter": "4", "verse": "2", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "When through abandoning delusion one knows the self by the self in the self, that alone is his conduct, his knowledge and his view.", "tags": {"level": "ultimate", "standpoint": "substance", "naya": "niscaya", "path": ["knowledge", "meditation"], "stage": "advanced"}, "types": ["ultimate", "practice"], "terms": ["trm:atman", "trm:ratnatraya"], "concepts": ["cpt:three-jewels", "cpt:niscaya-vyavahara"], "teachers": ["tch:hemacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "Verse number recalled, not checked; the content is the unit author's recollection (skeleton).", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yogasastra-hemacandra:4.5", "source": "src:yogasastra-hemacandra", "location": {"ref": "4.5", "chapter": "4", "verse": "5", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "This very self, conquered by the passions and senses, is worldly existence; the same self, their conqueror, the wise call liberation.", "tags": {"level": "bridging", "standpoint": "substance", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge", "action"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["karma-liberation", "ultimate"], "terms": ["trm:atman", "trm:moksa", "trm:kasaya"], "concepts": ["cpt:moksa-jain"], "teachers": ["tch:hemacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "Verse number recalled, not checked; the content is the unit author's recollection (skeleton).", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yogasastra-hemacandra:6.1", "source": "src:yogasastra-hemacandra", "location": {"ref": "6.1", "chapter": "6", "verse": "1", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "Entering another's body is described only as a wonder: it may not succeed even with great effort over a long time, and it is of no use for liberation.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "seeker", "naya": null, "path": ["body-breath"], "stage": "advanced"}, "types": ["powers-experiences"], "terms": ["trm:parasarira-avesa"], "obstacles": ["obs:labdhi-attachment"], "teachers": ["tch:hemacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "Verse number recalled, not checked; the content is the unit author's recollection (skeleton).", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yogasastra-hemacandra:6.4-5", "source": "src:yogasastra-hemacandra", "location": {"ref": "6.4-5", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "The mind tormented by breath-control does not attain well-being: restraining the breath causes pain, pain disturbs the mind; filling, holding and expelling the breath are laborious and distract, so breath-control is not a means to liberation.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "polemical", "naya": null, "path": ["body-breath", "meditation"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["practice", "dispute"], "terms": ["trm:pranayama"], "practices": ["prc:pranayama"], "teachers": ["tch:hemacandra"], "disputes": ["dsp:pranayama-and-liberation"], "cross_refs": ["tea:yoga-sutra:2.49", "tea:yoga-sutra:2.52"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "The text's own warning about breath-control. Verse number recalled, not checked; the content is the unit author's recollection (skeleton).", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yogasastra-hemacandra:7.8", "source": "src:yogasastra-hemacandra", "location": {"ref": "7.8", "chapter": "7", "verse": "8", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "The objects of meditation are declared fourfold: the embodied (piṇḍastha), the word-based (padastha), the form-based (rūpastha) and the formless (rūpātīta).", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "analytic", "naya": null, "path": ["meditation"], "stage": "intermediate"}, "types": ["practice"], "terms": ["trm:pindastha", "trm:padastha", "trm:rupastha", "trm:rupatita"], "concepts": ["cpt:four-dhyeyas"], "paths": ["pth:jain-four-dhyeyas"], "teachers": ["tch:hemacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "Verse number recalled, not checked; the content is the unit author's recollection (skeleton).", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yogasastra-hemacandra:7.9-28", "source": "src:yogasastra-hemacandra", "location": {"ref": "7.9-28", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "In embodied meditation there are five concentrations: of earth (visualising an ocean of milk, a lotus and oneself seated on Meru), of fire (the syllable 'arhaṃ' blazing up and burning the eight-petalled lotus of the eight karmas and the body), of wind (blowing the ashes away), of water (a rain washing away what remains), and the 'essential' one (seeing the self as pure, omniscient, like the Siddha).", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "experiential", "naya": null, "path": ["meditation", "sound"], "stage": "advanced"}, "types": ["practice", "powers-experiences"], "terms": ["trm:parthivi-dharana", "trm:agneyi-dharana", "trm:maruti-dharana", "trm:varuni-dharana", "trm:tattvabhu-dharana", "trm:arham"], "concepts": ["cpt:five-dharanas-jain"], "practices": ["prc:pindastha-dhyana", "prc:five-dharanas-jain"], "paths": ["pth:jain-four-dhyeyas"], "teachers": ["tch:hemacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "Visualisation contents recalled in outline; verse range not checked.", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yogasastra-hemacandra:ch.10", "source": "src:yogasastra-hemacandra", "location": {"ref": "ch.10", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "Virtuous meditation is practised as the four examinations — of the Jina's command, of the harm of passions, of the fruition of karma and of the structure of the universe — and its fruit is heavenly birth and, in time, liberation.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "seeker", "naya": null, "path": ["meditation"], "stage": "intermediate"}, "types": ["practice", "karma-liberation"], "terms": ["trm:dharma-dhyana", "trm:ajna-vicaya", "trm:apaya-vicaya", "trm:vipaka-vicaya", "trm:samsthana-vicaya"], "practices": ["prc:dharma-dhyana"], "teachers": ["tch:hemacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yogasastra-hemacandra:ch.11", "source": "src:yogasastra-hemacandra", "location": {"ref": "ch.11", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "Pure meditation, in its four stages, belongs to those of the best bodily frame who know the Pūrvas and to the omniscient; in this age it is described so that its nature may be known.", "tags": {"level": "ultimate", "standpoint": "experiential", "naya": null, "path": ["meditation"], "stage": "realized"}, "types": ["practice", "karma-liberation"], "terms": ["trm:sukla-dhyana"], "practices": ["prc:sukla-dhyana"], "paths": ["pth:jain-dhyana-ascent"], "teachers": ["tch:hemacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yogasastra-hemacandra:ch.12", "source": "src:yogasastra-hemacandra", "location": {"ref": "ch.12", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "When the mind is left alone in indifference (audāsīnya), without being forcibly restrained, it becomes still of itself; the more it is forced, the more it runs; in the no-mind state (unmanībhāva) the self is realised and the yogin, like one freed of a load, experiences bliss.", "tags": {"level": "ultimate", "standpoint": "experiential", "naya": null, "path": ["meditation"], "stage": "advanced"}, "types": ["consciousness-mind", "powers-experiences"], "terms": ["trm:audasinya", "trm:unmanibhava", "trm:svanubhuti"], "concepts": ["cpt:four-states-of-mind-hemacandra"], "teachers": ["tch:hemacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "Chapter-level summary from memory.", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yogasastra-hemacandra:ch.5", "source": "src:yogasastra-hemacandra", "location": {"ref": "ch.5", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "Breath-control, the five winds and their seats, the four 'circles' (maṇḍala) of the breath, and the signs by which the time of death can be foreknown are described, together with the practice of entering another body.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "analytic", "naya": null, "path": ["body-breath"], "stage": "advanced"}, "types": ["body-layers", "death-dying", "powers-experiences"], "terms": ["trm:prana-vayu-jain", "trm:kalajnana"], "practices": ["prc:pranayama", "prc:kalajnana"], "teachers": ["tch:hemacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "restricted": true, "notes": "Restricted: summary only (breath retention, death-omens). See 6.1 and 6.4–5 for Hemacandra's own warnings.", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yogasastra-hemacandra:ch.8", "source": "src:yogasastra-hemacandra", "location": {"ref": "ch.8", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "Word-based meditation rests on sacred syllables: the vowels and consonants placed on lotuses in the navel, heart and mouth; the syllable 'arhaṃ'; the five-fold homage to the supreme beings and its shorter forms; and the teacher's mantras, practised with purity and for liberation, not worldly ends.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "seeker", "naya": null, "path": ["meditation", "sound"], "stage": "advanced"}, "types": ["practice", "sound-language"], "terms": ["trm:padastha", "trm:arham", "trm:namaskara-mantra"], "practices": ["prc:padastha-dhyana", "prc:namaskara-japa", "prc:arham-dhyana"], "paths": ["pth:jain-four-dhyeyas"], "teachers": ["tch:hemacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yogasastra-hemacandra:ch.9", "source": "src:yogasastra-hemacandra", "location": {"ref": "ch.9", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "Form-based meditation contemplates the Arhat seated in the assembly hall (samavasaraṇa), radiant with his marvels; meditation on deities in passionate or fearsome forms is to be avoided, since one becomes what one meditates on.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "seeker", "naya": null, "path": ["meditation", "devotion"], "stage": "advanced"}, "types": ["practice"], "terms": ["trm:rupastha", "trm:samavasarana"], "practices": ["prc:rupastha-dhyana"], "obstacles": ["obs:asad-dhyana"], "paths": ["pth:jain-four-dhyeyas"], "teachers": ["tch:hemacandra"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
